<div class="na-layout"><div class="container container-1230"><div class="row g-4 align-items-start">
    <div class="col-lg-9">
        <article class="na-article">
            <p class="na-lead">The foreign exchange market — Forex or FX — is the largest and most liquid financial market on earth, with more than $7.5 trillion changing hands every single day. Yet its core idea is simple: you exchange one currency for another, hoping the one you hold rises in value. This guide breaks the essentials down into plain English so you can start with confidence.</p>

            <h2 class="na-h2">What Is the Forex Market?</h2>
            <p class="na-p">Unlike the stock market, Forex has no central exchange. It runs "over the counter" (OTC) across a global network of banks, brokers, institutions and individual traders. Because those participants span every time zone, the market stays open 24 hours a day, five days a week — from the Sydney open on Monday morning to the New York close on Friday evening. That constant availability is one of the reasons Forex is so accessible to part-time and full-time traders alike.</p>

            <h2 class="na-h2">How a Currency Pair Works</h2>
            <p class="na-p">Every Forex trade involves two currencies at once — you buy one while selling the other. That's why prices are always quoted as a <strong>pair</strong>, such as EUR/USD. The first currency is the <em>base</em>; the second is the <em>quote</em>. If EUR/USD is trading at 1.0850, it means one euro is worth 1.0850 US dollars.</p>
            <ul class="na-list">
                <li><strong>Major pairs</strong> always include the US dollar — EUR/USD, GBP/USD, USD/JPY. They have the tightest spreads and deepest liquidity.</li>
                <li><strong>Minor pairs</strong> (crosses) exclude the USD — for example EUR/GBP or AUD/JPY.</li>
                <li><strong>Exotic pairs</strong> combine a major currency with one from a smaller economy, like USD/TRY. They move sharply and carry wider spreads.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="na-h2">Key Terms Every Beginner Must Know</h2>
            <div class="na-step-grid">
                <div class="na-step"><div class="na-step-num" style="background:@al;color:@ac;">01</div><div><div class="na-step-title">Pips</div><div class="na-step-text">A pip is the smallest standard price move, usually the 4th decimal place (0.0001). If EUR/USD moves from 1.0850 to 1.0851, that's one pip. Pips are how you measure profit and loss.</div></div></div>
                <div class="na-step"><div class="na-step-num" style="background:@al;color:@ac;">02</div><div><div class="na-step-title">Lots</div><div class="na-step-text">Trade size is measured in lots. A standard lot is 100,000 units, a mini lot 10,000, and a micro lot just 1,000 — letting beginners start small and control risk.</div></div></div>
                <div class="na-step"><div class="na-step-num" style="background:@al;color:@ac;">03</div><div><div class="na-step-title">Leverage &amp; Margin</div><div class="na-step-text">Leverage lets you control a large position with a small deposit (margin). At 1:100, $1,000 controls $100,000. It magnifies profits — and losses — so use it carefully.</div></div></div>
                <div class="na-step"><div class="na-step-num" style="background:@al;color:@ac;">04</div><div><div class="na-step-title">Spread</div><div class="na-step-text">The spread is the small gap between the buy (ask) and sell (bid) price — effectively the broker's fee. Tighter spreads mean lower trading costs, which matters most for active traders.</div></div></div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="na-h2">The Four Trading Sessions</h2>
            <div class="row g-3 mb-3">
                @foreach(var s in new[]{
                    new{N="Sydney",T="22:00–07:00 UTC",D="The quiet opener of the trading week. Lower volatility, ideal for range strategies on AUD and NZD pairs.",Bg="#eff6ff",Clr="#2563eb"},
                    new{N="Tokyo",T="00:00–09:00 UTC",D="The Asian session. JPY pairs are most active. Steady, orderly moves suit patient entries.",Bg="#f0fdf4",Clr="#16a34a"},
                    new{N="London",T="07:00–16:00 UTC",D="The busiest session globally. Huge liquidity and sharp moves on EUR and GBP pairs.",Bg="#fffbeb",Clr="#d97706"},
                    new{N="New York",T="13:00–22:00 UTC",D="USD-driven. Major economic data lands here. The London–NY overlap is the peak window.",Bg="#fdf2f8",Clr="#db2777"}
                })
